From: =?UTF-8?q?S=C3=A9bastien=20Celles?= <s.celles@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 6 Jun 2026 13:20:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 13/38] feat(verimark): 3-attempt verify retry loop with
 WARM-mode-on-retry
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Port of the python verify retry loop (python/verimark/match.py:main).
Attempt 1 uses the strict RICH config that requires high-quality
touches ; attempts 2-3 prepend a 0x39 WARM that flips the firmware
into "retry mode" where (a) the on-chip pre-filter is loosened so
marginal touches get accepted, and (b) any type=0x18 event qualifies
as frame_ready, not just ones with the 0x1000 bit.

Also adds two latency improvements from the python reference :

- 7-opcode inter-attempt drain (mirrors _inter_attempt_drain), with
  cursors advancing by 4 per drain (2,3 ; 6,7 ; …).
- RICH-mode early-exit on `saw_false_detect` : a type=0x18 event
  without the FRAME_READY bit means the pre-filter has rejected the
  touch and no further FRAME_READY will fire for this attempt.
  Break the poll loop immediately and re-arm in WARM mode instead of
  waiting the full 30 s timeout.

Closes the long-standing reliability issue where the strict on-chip
pre-filter rejects ~50% of touches in single-attempt mode (observed
runtime ratio on WULFENIX). v1's "no-match on first attempt"
behaviour was driven entirely by this : 0x99 returned 0x0905 because
the captured frame was the marginal one the pre-filter would have
rejected in attempt 2's WARM mode.

Total worst-case latency : 3 × 30 s = 90 s (up from 30 s), but real
attempts succeed in ~1–3 s and retries only fire on actual rejections.

Drafted with AI assistance.
